{
  "dom_id": "step-q314291",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Find the slope $m$ of $y=g(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ if $g(x)=-4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+6.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the derivative using the product rule and sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(-4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{d}{dx}(-4{e}^{x})\\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡x+(-4{e}^{x})\\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+\\frac{d}{dx}(6) \\\\  & =-4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+(-4{e}^{x})(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)+0 \\\\  & =-4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\  & =-4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x(1-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and evaluate the slope $m:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-4{e}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(1-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =-4{e}^{\\pi /2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [
    {
